  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how natural organic matter (NOM) can change the environmental behavior of different nanomaterials, comparing carbon nanotubes (CNTs), nano-ZnO, and silver nanoparticles (AgNPs). At the center, a radar-style plot contrasts these materials across several fate and transformation processes, suggesting that each nanomaterial is influenced by NOM in a different pattern and to a different extent.\n\nAround the plot, the main visible mechanisms are illustrated. NOM can cause particle agglomeration or aggregation, including tangled clustering for CNTs, and can also promote deposition onto surfaces. It may passivate particle surfaces either by chelating molecules coating the surface or through chemical reactions that lead to dissolution and release of metal ions. Another pathway shown is surface oxidation or complexation, indicated by oxygen-containing functional groups. The figure also highlights microbially enhanced dissolution or formation, implying interactions with microorganisms can alter particle chemistry, and bioaccumulation in organisms is shown as an additional outcome.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that NOM strongly controls nanoparticle stability, transformation, transport, and biological uptake, with material-specific effects that appear distinct for CNTs, ZnO, and Ag-based particles.",
